items.text:
This site itself is one instance of "building what you've understood." The requirements for the stack were simple:
Content should be editable in the backend, without touching code every time;
The front end should be fast and stable, ideally purely static, with no server to maintain;
Data should stay in your own hands, with no lock-in when you migrate.
The final answer is:EmDash CMS + Astro + static publishing.
What is EmDash
EmDash is a full-stack TypeScript CMS built on Astro, called WordPress's 'spiritual successor': posts, pages, categories, tags, menus, media library, author bylines — it has all of WordPress's core concepts, but everything runs on a type-safe modern foundationUp.
The database is SQLite (with Postgres, Turso and Cloudflare D1 also supported)—one file is the entire content library;
The admin is a ready-made management interface with drafts, revision history, and scheduled publishing;
You can add fields to the content model right in the admin, no code changes needed;
Login uses Passkey by default, and also supports OAuth.
Key Decision: Admin Runs Locally, the Front End Is Static
EmDash's backend and API need a server runtime, but a personal blog doesn't want to keep a server just for the backend. My approach:
Run npm run dev locally to start the Astro dev server; the admin is at /_emdash/admin—writing posts, uploading images, and editing fields all happen there;
Every front-end page is marked for prerendering; at build time the content is read from SQLite and turned into pure static HTML;
Publish the static build to Qoder Sites: zero servers, zero database…
